::ENG3_U04_L03_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Research question: How does the image of an empty town square develop community memory? Passage: The fountain is dry, but the stone rim is smooth from many hands. Source: Public monuments in literature often preserve shared history through physical traces. Which synthesis is strongest? {
~The fountain is dry, and public monuments are in literature.#This only places facts side by side.
~The stone rim has been touched by many hands, so the town should repair the fountain.#This shifts to a practical recommendation.
=The smooth stone rim supports the idea of community memory because the source explains that physical traces on monuments can preserve shared history.#Correct. This combines source information to support the interpretation.
~The source proves that all fountains are monuments.#This overgeneralizes the source.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source: Literary settings can suggest history when objects show evidence of repeated human use. Which paraphrase is most accurate? {
=A setting may reveal the past when objects show signs that people used them over time.#Correct. This restates the idea accurately.
~Objects in literature are only important when many people use them.#This adds an unsupported limitation.
~Human use ruins objects in literary settings.#This changes the meaning of the source.
~Settings are more important than characters in historical literature.#This introduces a comparison the source does not make.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: A theater stage is dark, but circles on the floor show where performers once stood. Source: Performance spaces can preserve public memory by holding traces of past voices and audiences. Which sentence best synthesizes the sources? {
~The stage is dark, and performance spaces are important.#This is too general and does not explain the connection.
~The performers stood in circles on the stage floor.#This misreads the detail as performers standing in circles.
~The source is about voices, while the passage is about a stage.#This separates the sources instead of connecting them.
=The floor marks support an interpretation about public memory because the research source explains that performance spaces preserve traces of past voices and audiences.#Correct. This connects primary evidence and secondary context.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Passage: A closed station has dusty ticket windows and benches worn smooth by travelers. Source: Transit spaces in literature can symbolize transition, loss, and connection. Which analytical sentence uses both sources well? {
~The ticket windows are dusty, and travelers used the benches.#This summarizes the passage only.
=The station suggests both loss and connection because its abandoned windows show closure while the worn benches support the source's idea that transit spaces hold traces of shared movement.#Correct. This uses both sources to support analysis.
~Transit spaces are symbols, so the station must be the most important setting in the story.#This overstates the source and adds an unsupported claim.
~The benches are smooth because people sat on them.#This explains the literal detail without analyzing it.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Research question: How does a family quilt develop the theme of inheritance? Which secondary source best supports the question? {
~A shopping guide comparing modern blanket prices.#This does not support literary interpretation.
~A biography of an unrelated athlete who owned a quilt.#This is not connected to the literary theme.
=An article about handmade objects as symbols of family memory in literature.#Correct. This source connects directly to the object and theme.
~A weather report about cold winters.#This is not useful for the literary question.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The article explains public spaces preserve memory the poem shows a worn bench. Which revision is clearest? {
=The article explains that public spaces can preserve memory, and the poem shows a worn bench as evidence of past community life.#Correct. This uses a complete sentence and clear connection.
~The article explains public spaces preserve memory, the poem showing a worn bench.#This is not a complete sentence.
~Because the article explains public spaces and the poem shows a worn bench.#This is a fragment.
~The article explains public spaces preserve memory the poem shows a worn bench, and evidence.#This is a run-on and unclear.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: A speaker touches a carved name on an old school desk. Source: Scholars argue that inscriptions in literature can reveal a desire to be remembered. Which response best synthesizes? {
~The desk is old, and scholars write about inscriptions.#This only lists information.
=The carved name supports an interpretation about memory because the source explains that inscriptions can show a person's desire to be remembered.#Correct. This explains how the source helps interpret the literary detail.
~The speaker should not touch the carved name because it might be fragile.#This gives advice instead of analysis.
~The source is stronger than the passage because scholars wrote it.#This evaluates source authority without synthesis.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source: Objects that seem ordinary may become symbolic when characters connect them to shared history. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~Ordinary objects are never symbolic unless they are valuable.#This adds a condition not in the source.
~Characters should avoid ordinary objects because they are not historical.#This contradicts the source.
~Shared history is always more important than character development.#This adds an unsupported comparison.
=Everyday objects can gain symbolic meaning when characters link them to a community's past.#Correct. This keeps the meaning and uses new wording.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Research question: How does a broken clock develop the theme of being trapped in the past? Which evidence pairing is best? {
~The clock's brand name and a website selling antique clocks.#This is not focused on literary interpretation.
=A detail showing the clock stopped years ago and a source about stopped time as a symbol of memory in literature.#Correct. Both pieces directly support the research question.
~The room's paint color and a source about interior design.#This does not address the clock or theme.
~The author's birth year and a map of the town.#These may provide context but do not directly answer the question.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Passage: The narrator keeps a rusted key after the house is gone. Source: Keys often symbolize access, secrecy, or memory. Which sentence best uses the source in analysis? {
=The rusted key suggests memory rather than access because the house is gone, and the source helps explain why a key can still carry symbolic meaning.#Correct. This makes an interpretation and uses the source to explain it.
~The key is rusted, so it probably cannot open a lock anymore.#This is literal and does not use the source analytically.
~Keys symbolize access, secrecy, or memory in many texts.#This repeats the source without applying it to the passage.
~The narrator keeps a key after the house is gone.#This is summary only.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Scenario: A student writes, The bridge is broken. The article says bridges connect people. What is the main weakness? {
~The sentence uses a source that is too scholarly.#There is no evidence that the source is too scholarly.
~The sentence mentions the article after the passage.#Order is not the main problem.
~The sentence uses a bridge as evidence.#The bridge can be relevant evidence.
=The sentence does not explain how the broken bridge and the article support one interpretation.#Correct. It lists source ideas without synthesizing them.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: A town sign is faded, but people still leave flowers below it. Source: Memorial objects can help communities turn private grief into public remembrance. Which synthesis is strongest? {
~The sign is faded, and people leave flowers.#This is summary only.
=The faded sign and flowers support an interpretation about public remembrance because the source explains that memorial objects can make grief communal.#Correct. This connects both sources to the interpretation.
~The town sign should be repainted so people can read it.#This is a recommendation, not synthesis.
~The source is about grief, but the passage is about flowers.#This separates connected ideas.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source: A secondary source should clarify the literary question rather than distract from it with unrelated facts. Which paraphrase is best? {
=A useful research source should help answer the interpretation question and avoid irrelevant information.#Correct. This captures the source idea accurately.
~Secondary sources should always be shorter than primary sources.#This is not stated.
~Unrelated facts are better because they make research broader.#This contradicts the source.
~Every literary question requires at least five sources.#This adds an unsupported requirement.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence uses punctuation correctly in a synthesis statement? {
~The poem shows a cracked doorway the article explains thresholds in literature.#This is a run-on sentence.
~The poem shows a cracked doorway, the article explains thresholds in literature.#This comma splice is incorrect.
=The poem shows a cracked doorway, and the article explains how thresholds can symbolize change in literature.#Correct. The coordinating conjunction joins the ideas clearly.
~The poem showing a cracked doorway and the article explaining thresholds.#This is a fragment.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: A character returns to an empty dock where ropes still hang from the posts. Source: Waterfront settings can symbolize departure and return. Which interpretation is best supported by both sources? {
~The dock needs repairs because ropes are hanging from it.#This is literal and not interpretive.
~The ropes are more important than the character.#This is unsupported.
~The source is about waterfront settings, so the dock must be beautiful.#This adds an unsupported idea.
=The empty dock suggests unresolved return because the remaining ropes connect the setting to departure and return.#Correct. This uses both the passage and source.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Scenario: A student wants to write a synthesis sentence about a burned field in a story and a source about renewal imagery. Which sentence is strongest? {
~The field burned, and renewal imagery is common.#This lists ideas without explaining meaning.
=The burned field can support a theme of renewal if later growth appears, and the source explains why destruction imagery can prepare readers for rebirth.#Correct. This connects source context to a conditional literary interpretation.
~The source is about renewal, so the story must have a happy ending.#This overstates what the source can prove.
~The field is burned, so the author dislikes farming.#This invents author attitude.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: A deserted church still echoes when the narrator speaks. Source: Echoes in literature can suggest the persistence of absent voices. Which synthesis is strongest? {
~The narrator speaks inside a church.#This uses only the passage.
~The source discusses echoes in literature.#This uses only the source.
=The church echo supports an interpretation about absence because the source explains that echoes can suggest voices that remain after people are gone.#Correct. This synthesizes the passage and source.
~The narrator should speak louder because the church is empty.#This is practical advice, not analysis.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source: Context should be used to illuminate a literary interpretation, not replace close reading of the text. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=Background information should help explain the literary text, but it should not take the place of analyzing textual details.#Correct. This keeps the source meaning.
~Context is more important than close reading in every literary analysis.#This reverses the source's point.
~Close reading should be avoided when context is available.#This contradicts the source.
~Literary interpretations do not need background information.#This overstates the idea.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Research question: How does a storm-damaged tree develop a theme about endurance? Which source is least useful? {
~The passage describing the tree still standing after the storm.#This is primary evidence and directly useful.
~An article about trees as endurance symbols in literature.#This directly supports the interpretation.
~A source explaining how storms can create conflict in narrative settings.#This can help interpret the storm.
=A gardening manual listing fertilizer schedules for fruit trees.#Correct. This is unrelated to the literary theme and interpretation.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Passage: A character carries a cracked mirror wrapped in cloth. Source: Mirrors often invite questions about identity and self-perception. Which sentence best supports analysis? {
~The mirror is cracked and wrapped in cloth.#This is just evidence, not analysis.
=The cracked mirror can symbolize a damaged sense of identity because the source explains that mirrors often connect to self-perception.#Correct. This applies the source to the passage's symbol.
~Mirrors are common objects in houses and stories.#This is too general.
~The cloth is probably soft so the mirror will not break more.#This is literal speculation.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The source says windows can symbolize perspective and the poem shows a boarded window which suggests blocked understanding. Which revision is clearest? {
=The source explains that windows can symbolize perspective, and the poem's boarded window suggests blocked understanding.#Correct. This revision is clear and grammatically complete.
~The source explains windows can symbolize perspective, the poem's boarded window suggesting blocked understanding.#This is a comma splice and fragment.
~Because windows can symbolize perspective and a boarded window.#This is incomplete.
~The poem's boarded window, and the source explains perspective, blocked understanding.#This is not a clear sentence.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: A village well is sealed, but people gather beside it every morning. Source: Shared rituals in literature can preserve community even when their original purpose changes. Which synthesis is strongest? {
~The well is sealed, but people gather there.#This summarizes only the passage.
~The source explains shared rituals.#This states only the source.
=The sealed well still supports community because the passage shows people gathering there, and the source explains that rituals can preserve community after their original purpose changes.#Correct. This connects both sources to one interpretation.
~The well is useless because it is sealed.#This ignores the symbolic and communal meaning.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source: Synthesis requires explaining relationships among sources, not simply arranging evidence in the same paragraph. Which paraphrase is best? {
~Synthesis means putting many quotations in one paragraph.#This directly contradicts the source.
~Sources should always be arranged from oldest to newest.#This adds an unsupported rule.
~Evidence becomes synthesis automatically when it is in one paragraph.#This contradicts the source.
=To synthesize, a writer must explain how sources connect rather than merely place them together.#Correct. This accurately restates the source.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Scenario: A student has a poem about an abandoned marketplace and sources about economics, architecture, and community gathering spaces. Which source best supports a literary interpretation about lost community? {
=The source about community gathering spaces.#Correct. It connects most directly to the interpretation about community.
~The source about currency exchange rates.#This is economics-related but not focused on the literary interpretation.
~The source about roof construction.#This is architectural detail, not the theme of community.
~The source about market taxes.#This is unlikely to help interpret lost community in the poem.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Passage: A character keeps sweeping an empty porch. Source: Domestic spaces can represent belonging and memory. Which analysis best synthesizes the sources? {
~The character sweeps because the porch is dirty.#This is literal and unsupported.
=The repeated sweeping suggests a desire to preserve belonging because the source explains that domestic spaces can carry memory.#Correct. This connects action, setting, and source context.
~The porch is empty, so the character should leave.#This gives advice instead of analysis.
~Domestic spaces are always happier than public spaces.#This overgeneralizes beyond the source.
}
